n(x for y, x in cells)\n                right = max(x for y, x in cells)\n                boxes.append((top, bottom, left, right, color))\n    out = [[bg] * w for _ in range(h)]\n    for top, bottom, left, right, color in boxes:\n        for y in range(top, bottom + 1):\n            for x in range(left, right + 1):\n                out[y][x] = color\n    return out\n"},{"type":"reasoning","text":"**Evaluating file transformation**\n\nI need to run a transformation on an existing file before submitting it. There’s also a skill requirement that seems to demand a continual process of improvement. It’s good to read through everything carefully to ensure I understand what’s expected. I'll keep my focus on making sure each step is carried out correctly, so I meet those requirements without missing anything crucial. It feels important to get this right!","t":1792557314.778,"summary":true},{"type":"tool","name":"read","input":"{\n \"optional\": true,\n \"path\": \"/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-openclawlearner-1792497764/attempts/ff04010ea19b4e2796afd219b7bae1c0/system/state/openclaw/agents/main/agent/workshop-skills/continual-arc/SKILL.md\",\n \"offset\": 1,\n \"limit\": 200,\n \"cursor\": 0\n}","t":1792557314.778,"recalled":true,"output":"---\nname: continual-arc\ndescription: Solve Continual-ARC grids by applying saved task transforms, learning from demos, and submitting verified outputs.\n---\n\n# Continual-ARC\n\n1. At instance start, inspect `programs/<task id>.py` in the specified workspace. If present, run its `transform(test_grid)` and immediately submit that exact result only if it returns a valid rectangular grid of values 0–9; if unusable or absent, request a demo before coding.\n2. Transcribe every complete demo pair exactly and keep inputs/outputs for regression checks. Infer the rule from specific structures shown, not a global visual hunch; request another demo when it can distinguish plausible rules.\n3. After every program edit, execute the exact transform on every received demo input and require exact grid equality with each output before submitting. A wrong submission is evidence against the rule, not a prompt to repeat it: revisit the demos and identify the triggering structure and direction of each change. For ray/extension rules, explicitly check which object sends the continuation and where it stops; do not fill every row merely because it has foreground cells, nor add a companion/backward fill unless examples verify that behavior. If demo cap is exhausted without a verified rule, don't claim the guess is verified.\n4. Run the verified transform on the full test grid, assert dimensions, rectangularity, and cell range, then submit exactly its produced grid and no other payload. Save/replace the task file only after a correct verdict; if no transform reproduces all demos, preserve any prior file.\n5. End every turn with exactly one valid action JSON. Request demos when evidence is needed; submit only an output from the verified transform; use `finish` only after the instance is over. For binary recoloring tasks using color 5 and one alternate color `x`, test `5 -> x` and `x -> 0` against every demo; do not assume identity or generalize from a stale palette-specific branch.\n"},{"type":"tool","name":"exec","input":"python - <<'PY'\nfrom importlib.util import spec_from_file_location,module_from_spec\np='/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-openclawlearner-1792497764/attempts/ff04010ea19b4e2796afd219b7bae1c0/system/state/workspace/programs/task-48b289ca.py'\ns=spec_from_file_location('m',p);m=module_from_spec(s);s.loader.exec_module(m)\ng=[[3,8,8,8,7,5,7,7,7,7,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,7,7,7,3,7,7,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,2,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,7,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,7,7,2,7,7,7,8,8,9],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,9,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,9,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,2,8],[8,8,8,5,8,8,8,8,8,5,7,7,7],[8,8,8,8,8,3,8,8,8,8,7,7,7],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,7,7,